Z||000000||next level trends prez||12-20-2001||10:43 PM||||i bled out the system and bled it out.. i got good pressure but the  i rove to the store down the road the around the block to make sure it was ok... i shut it off then came back out two hours later to go to my club meeting and i went to back out got to the road and the clutch went to the floor with no pressure.. what gives? then i pumped it a couple times and it was ok then it went out again... i did that a couple times trying to get back into my driveway then it had no pressure at all and still does not..could my slave cylinder have went bad.. how do i know ...i just got the car and im getting really pissed off if it not one probablem it another.. and as i read it seems a lot of people are haveing probablems with there clutch in the 88s... there has to be someone who knows how to fix our probablems||||reg||1
Z||000001||REDHOTT88||12-20-2001||10:51 PM||||PROBABLEM??  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I've never had any clutch problems in my 88 Formula... maybe I'm lucky, but mine has been flawless.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Keep looking in the search area, try different keywords... someone will post some advice here pretty soon. Hang in there!!<P>------------------<BR>                                                                                                         <BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/FIREdevil.gif">           <FONT COLOR="ffoooo"> <B>1988 Formula 2.8L</B> V6 5 speed  </FONT><BR><FONT SIZE="1">(Runs smooth, shifts crisp, red and shiny all over!)</FONT><p>[This message has been edited by REDHOTT88 (edited 12-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfiero||12-20-2001||10:57 PM||||You probably have a bad master and/or slave cylinder.<P>Remove the two 13mm nuts that hold the slave to the tranny and pop off the rubber boot.  Look for ANY signs of fluid.  There is some grease inside the rubber boot, so don't mistake that for fluid.  Any fluid means a bad slave.  Only replace with a GM slave......DO NOT buy one from NAPA, Auto Zone or any other parts house.  Don't listen to anyone who tells you otherwise.....your life will be much easier if you get the GM unit.<P>The master cylinder is also easy to check.  Crawl under the dash and find the rod that attaches the clutch pedal to the master cylinder (goes through firewall).  Pop off the rubber boot and again check for signs of fluid.  Replace the master with a genuine GM part if you see/feel any fluid.<P>You might also just have air in the lines and need to rebleed the system.  The easiest way to bleed the system is to remove the slave from the tranny and open the bleeder.......with the bleeder open push in on the rod.  This forces the air/fluid out of the slave.  With the rod pushed in, hold it until you retighten the bleeder.  Repeat a few times.<P>Is the slave in your car black or cast iron (probably rusty if it's cast)?  That will tell you what you have.......the cast iron units are aftermarket and are junk.  Again, replace with GM parts only.<P>Good luck!     ||||reg||1
Z||000003||DRH||12-20-2001||10:58 PM||||I just posted some things to look at a couple of threads down. Look here:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/016550.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/016550.html</A> <P>See if what I posted helps narrow it down a little more. It's not just 88s, all Fiero clutches have the same problems or sometimes unexplained years of trouble free service.<BR>||||reg||1
